add('consulting-business-plan','Consulting Business Plan: Scope, Delivery Hours & Cash','Write a consulting business plan around a clear client problem, accepted deliverables, project hours, retainer capacity and payment timing. Includes an original worked model.','Independent consultant reviewing client work folders and a paper capacity calendar',[
('Define the consulting promise and acceptance evidence',p('A consulting business plan should explain which client problem you solve, the bounded result you deliver, who accepts it and what evidence supports demand. This guide models a small advisory practice with fixed-fee projects and limited retainers. It does not assume that an interest call is a signed engagement or that a persuasive proposal guarantees a result.')+p('For example, define a stock-record review for a particular kind of small retailer: a written findings report, an agreed number of meetings and a handover checklist. Identify what is excluded, what data the client supplies and what happens if that data arrives late. Record permission to use client information and separate public planning notes from confidential source files.')),
('Build one pipeline and a delivery-hour ceiling',p('Keep each opportunity under one ID with its current stage, next action, owner and decision date. A meeting, proposal and signed project can be stages of the same opportunity; adding those stage totals would overstate the pipeline. Validate demand with authorized conversations, written scope and actual paid pilots rather than generic market-size figures.')+table('Fictional monthly consulting capacity',['Line','Hours','Meaning'],[['Total working time','160','Entered allowance for the month'],['Administration','32','Not available for delivery'],['Business development','16','Separate from client work'],['Training and internal work','8','Reserved before booking'],['Direct delivery allowance','104','160 - 32 - 16 - 8'],['Three projects','72','3 x 24 hours'],['Two bounded retainers','20','2 x 10 hours'],['Unallocated delivery buffer','12','104 - 72 - 20']])+p('The 12-hour buffer is not another sale already made. If each project needs four extra hours, all 12 hours disappear. If each needs eight extra hours, total delivery rises to 116 hours and exceeds the allowance by 12. Retainers need a written hour or deliverable boundary; otherwise a fixed monthly fee can hide an unlimited service promise.')),
('Reconcile project and retainer economics',table('Illustrative monthly operating model',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Project fees','3 x 1,800','5,400'],['Retainer fees','2 x 500','1,000'],['Modeled revenue','5,400 + 1,000','6,400'],['Direct project costs','3 x 120','360'],['Direct retainer costs','2 x 40','80'],['Listed period overhead','Entered assumption','1,400'],['Remainder before owner pay and tax','6,400 - 440 - 1,400','4,560']])+p('The $1,800 and $500 fees are fictional negotiated inputs, not industry prices. The $4,560 remainder excludes owner compensation, tax and costs not listed. Do not call it take-home pay or compare it with another practice that includes owner salary. Keep subcontractor costs, reimbursed travel and client-paid software inside a consistently defined boundary.')+p('A project with the same fee but more revisions uses more capacity without automatically earning more income. Review change requests before committing extra hours. Use the <a href="/tools/fixed-fee-scope-change-planner/">scope-change worksheet</a> to record changed work and the <a href="/journal/agency-capacity-planning-example/">agency capacity example</a> for a broader team allocation.')),
('Put deposits and later balances on different dates',p('Assume each project collects a 50% deposit and both retainers are paid at the start of the month. Initial receipts are 3 x $900 + 2 x $500 = $3,700. The other $2,700 of project fees is expected after the agreed work and payment conditions. With $3,000 opening operating cash and all $1,840 of listed costs paid before those balances arrive, the intermediate balance is $4,860. It is not the eventual revenue total.')+p('If only two projects are delivered alongside the same two retainers, modeled revenue becomes $4,600. Direct costs fall to $320, while listed overhead stays $1,400; the remainder becomes $2,880 before the same omissions. Separately test a late balance: earned income can remain unchanged while the receipt moves into another month. A positive margin does not remove a cash deadline.')),
('Turn the plan into a bounded evidence test',p('Write a short customer definition, service scope, alternatives, lead channel, delivery calendar, fee basis, cash schedule and downside response. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> connects these sections; the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">editable template</a> captures the narrative. The <a href="https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/write-your-business-plan">SBA planning resource</a> is a starting point for choosing the document format.')+p('During a 30-day pilot, retain the original estimates and record actual preparation, meetings, analysis, revisions and collection dates. Set a spending ceiling and name the person who decides whether to continue, narrow the offer or stop. A consulting niche may involve regulated professional activity; verify the requirements for the actual work and location before offering it. This worksheet does not establish a credential or interpret a client contract.')+p('<strong>Should every available hour be billable?</strong> No; reserve the non-delivery commitments first. <strong>Is a retainer guaranteed recurring profit?</strong> No; renewal, scope, cost and collection remain assumptions. <strong>What belongs in an investor or lender version?</strong> Confirm the recipient\'s additional evidence and statement requirements rather than assuming this short model is sufficient.'))
],[SBA],REL)
add('insurance-agency-business-plan','Insurance Agency Business Plan: Policies, Renewals & Cash','Build an insurance agency business plan that separates premium volume, earned agency income, renewal cohorts, service capacity and commission cash. Includes a fictional example.','Insurance agency owner reviewing anonymous policy folders and a calculator',[
('Define the agency, permissions and revenue boundary',p('An insurance agency business plan must distinguish an agency or brokerage from an insurer that underwrites risk. Define the intended customer, lines of business, service area, carrier relationships, responsible people and permitted activities. This guide models a fictional agency operating within its actual permissions. It does not recommend an insurance product or establish permission to sell one.')+p('The <a href="https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/producer-licensing">NAIC producer-licensing overview</a> identifies state regulators as the relevant US licensing authorities. Check the requirements for the people, business entity, location and activities involved with the responsible state department. Record unresolved questions; a completed business plan is not a licensing decision.')),
('Separate policy cohorts from premium and agency income',table('Fictional period model; all rates are entered contract assumptions',['Book segment','Policies','Premium volume','Agency rate','Modeled agency income'],[['New business','40','40,000','12%','4,800'],['Renewals','80 of 100 due','50,000','8%','4,000'],['Combined','120 issued/renewed','90,000','Not one blended entitlement','8,800']])+p('The $90,000 is premium volume, not agency revenue or available operating cash. Renewal retention here is 80 / 100 = 80% for one closed cohort of policies due. It is not customer retention, premium retention or a forecast for every future month. Keep cancellations, rewrites and new policies separately so a rewritten policy is not counted twice.')+p('The 12% and 8% assumptions are invented for arithmetic. Actual compensation, fees, payment conditions, reversals and renewal rights depend on the agreements and applicable requirements. Do not use this example as a market rate, an entitlement to renewal income or a statement about what a producer may charge.')),
('Reserve service capacity before accepting growth',p('Assume 40 new policies need 1.25 hours each and 80 renewals need 0.5 hours each: 50 + 40 = 90 direct service hours. A 100-hour total allowance with 20 hours reserved for administration leaves only 80 direct hours. The plan is 10 hours over capacity. Faster marketing does not solve that service gap.')+p('One illustrative response is to defer eight new cases where commercially and contractually appropriate: 32 x 1.25 + 80 x 0.5 = 80 direct hours. New premium assumptions fall to $32,000 and modeled new-business income to $3,840. With $4,000 renewal income, total modeled income becomes $7,840. This shows the consequence of a capacity choice, not advice to abandon an individual policyholder. Establish qualified cover, service priorities and documented handoffs instead of promising unavailable response time.')+p('Keep a dated renewal register, missing-information list, responsible owner and next action. Store policyholder information in appropriately controlled systems. The <a href="/journal/crm-for-solopreneurs/">CRM workflow</a> illustrates next-action records but is not a policy-administration or carrier integration.')),
('Model reversals, costs and receipt timing separately',table('Original planning-only income-to-cash bridge',['Line','USD','Boundary'],[['Gross agency income assumption','8,800','From the first scenario'],['Planning allowance for potential adjustments','600','Fictional risk input, not an accounting rule'],['Income after modeled allowance','8,200','Before the costs below'],['Direct service costs','2,000','Entered assumption'],['Period overhead','3,800','Entered assumption'],['Modeled remainder','2,400','Before owner pay, tax and omitted costs']])+p('For the cash view, assume the agency has collected $6,000 of its own income, paid $5,800 of listed operating costs and starts with $7,000 of operating cash. The bank balance for this bounded operating example is $7,200. If the owner also earmarks $600 against possible later reversals, the unearmarked amount is $6,600. That earmark is a manual planning choice, not a statement of legally required reserves.')+p('Do not add premium money held for carriers or customers to this operating balance. Use the actual applicable handling requirements. Later statements can change income or create a repayment obligation; reconcile statements to receipts rather than assuming all quoted commission arrives immediately. The <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash-flow example</a> explains dated collections and payments.')),
('Set evidence, downside and review decisions',p('The plan should include a defined segment, permission and appointment checks, actual agreement evidence, acquisition costs, service capacity, renewal cohort definitions, reversal assumptions and dated cash commitments. Compare alternatives on documented service scope and actual requirements, not invented ratings or a claim that all agencies receive the same compensation.')+p('Test a small permitted segment and preserve the original expectations. Review cases awaiting information, service hours, renewals due, reversals and collected agency income. Name the reviewer and a spending or booking stop rule. Use the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> for evidence owners and the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">planning guide</a> for the wider document.')+p('<strong>Can premium volume be described as agency sales revenue?</strong> This example keeps it separate from agency income. <strong>Is an 80% renewal result a benchmark?</strong> No, it is one invented cohort. <strong>Does the worksheet replace regulatory or agreement review?</strong> No; those questions remain with the actual responsible parties.'))
],[SBA,NAIC],REL)
add('spa-business-plan','Spa Business Plan: Appointments, Room Capacity & Cash','Write a day-spa business plan using appointment lengths, staff and room capacity, no-shows, product costs and payment timing. Includes a worked example and downside case.','Day-spa owner reviewing appointment cards at a reception desk with folded linen',[
('Choose a bounded day-spa service model',p('A spa business plan starts with the type of operation: a day spa, a resort service and a clinical medical spa have different requirements and economics. This guide uses a fictional nonmedical day-spa appointment model. It does not describe medical treatments, health outcomes or the permissions for a particular service.')+p('Define the target guest, permitted treatment menu, appointment duration, preparation and turnover, qualified staff, premises and cancellation terms. Validate demand with authorized inquiries and a small permitted trial. A beautiful reception area is not evidence that enough appointments will be completed at the planned price. Record current alternatives and the specific service gap you can support.')),
('Use the tighter of room-time and staff-time',table('Fictional one-day capacity',['Resource','Calculation','Available minutes'],[['Two rooms','2 x 8 hours x 60','960'],['Two practitioners after other duties','2 x 6 hours x 60','720'],['One appointment including turnover','60 treatment + 15 turnaround','75'],['Whole appointments supported by rooms','Floor(960 / 75)','12 appointments'],['Whole appointments per practitioner','Floor(360 / 75)','4 appointments'],['Two-practitioner staffing ceiling','2 x 4','8 appointments']])+p('The staff-supported ceiling is eight whole appointments: each practitioner has 360 minutes and can deliver four 75-minute slots. Pooling both schedules and taking Floor(720 / 75) would suggest nine, but the ninth uninterrupted appointment cannot fit either individual practitioner. Eight is still an upper bound, not a booking recommendation: actual rosters, breaks, room suitability and guest needs can reduce it. More rooms do not create more qualified practitioner time.')+p('Assume eight appointments are booked and seven are completed; the eighth is an unfilled no-show. Completed appointments are 7 / 8 = 87.5% of bookings and use 525 of the 720 staff minutes, or 72.92%. That percentage is not room utilization. Keep denominator and completion status explicit. Use the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">weekly time-blocking template</a> to inspect the actual schedule rather than assuming the daily totals establish simultaneous coverage.')),
('Reconcile appointment contribution and listed day costs',table('Illustrative day economics',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Completed service revenue','7 x 90','630'],['Consumables','7 x 12','84'],['Laundry and turnover supplies','7 x 5','35'],['Payment fee assumption','630 x 3%','18.90'],['Scheduled staff cost','Entered day total','260'],['Premises and other period allocation','Entered day total','140'],['Remainder before owner pay and tax','630 - 84 - 35 - 18.90 - 260 - 140','92.10']])+p('The $90 price and 3% fee are fictional inputs, not typical prices or a processor offer. This model charges staff cost for the scheduled day, so a no-show does not automatically remove that cost. State whether an owner also provides treatments and whether their compensation is included. Add equipment, maintenance, insurance and other actual costs before relying on the plan.')+p('At only five completed appointments, revenue is $450. The same unit assumptions give $85 of consumables and laundry, $13.50 of fees and $400 of listed day costs. The remainder becomes -$48.50. Filling unused time matters only if the work is permitted, staffed and produces useful incremental contribution.')),
('Keep deposits, gift balances and completed sales distinct',p('Suppose $140 of deposits was collected before the reporting day and credited against the seven completed services. The remaining service receipts that day are $490. Revenue for the seven completed services is still $630 in this illustrative earned-service view; adding the deposits again would double-count it. The earlier deposits belong in the earlier cash schedule.')+p('With $1,000 opening day cash that already includes those deposits, $490 received and $537.90 of the listed costs paid that day, closing cash is $952.10. Timing differs from the $92.10 operating remainder. Prepaid gift balances for unperformed services need their own obligation record; do not assume every amount collected is already earned or freely withdrawable.')+p('Keep cancellations, refunds, unused balances and rescheduling conditions visible. This example does not determine the legal or accounting treatment of a particular gift card or deposit. Use the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash-flow guide</a> and actual recipient requirements for the wider financial plan.')),
('Verify the service and run a controlled review',p('Check the responsible authorities for the actual service and premises before opening. For example, <a href="https://www.tdlr.texas.gov/mas/apply-establishment.htm">Texas TDLR describes massage-establishment licensing and licensed practitioner requirements</a>. That example is Texas-specific and is not a universal spa checklist. Do not treat this nonmedical model as approval to offer clinical services.')+p('For a bounded four-week trial, record booked versus completed appointments, elapsed service and turnover time, qualified coverage, stock use, refunds and actual receipts. Review peak-time conflicts before adding rooms or extending hours. Keep the original assumptions and assign the next action, owner, due date and evidence. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> supplies the overall structure; the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">template</a> records it.')+p('<strong>Does eight-appointment capacity prove eight daily sales?</strong> No; capacity and demand are different. <strong>Should every deposit be added to revenue again?</strong> No; reconcile its application. <strong>Is this a medical-spa plan?</strong> No; that operation requires a separate professional and regulatory assessment.'))
],[SBA,TDLR],REL)
add('hotel-business-plan','Hotel Business Plan: Room Nights, Occupancy & Operating Cash','Build a small-hotel business plan with clear room-night denominators, occupancy, ADR, RevPAR, operating costs and receipt timing. Includes an original downside model.','Boutique hotel manager reviewing a paper room grid beside brass room-key hooks',[
('Specify the hotel and the reporting boundary',p('A hotel business plan should identify the guest segment, property format, room types, distribution channels, service level and operating period. This guide models a fictional 12-room independent hotel over a 30-day period. It is not a real property valuation, market demand study or financing offer.')+p('Document access, seasonality, competing alternatives, maintenance needs, staffing and the requirements for the actual premises. Interviews and dated local evidence can inform a test; a high occupancy target by itself does not demonstrate demand. Separate accommodation, food, events and other services so the units and revenue boundaries stay consistent. For a single hosted property, the <a href="/journal/short-term-rental-business-plan/">short-term rental guide</a> uses a different stay-and-turnover model.')),
('Reconcile room supply, sold nights and revenue metrics',table('Fictional 30-day room model',['Metric','Calculation','Result'],[['Gross physical room-nights','12 x 30','360'],['Unavailable maintenance nights','Entered assumption','30'],['Operating available room-nights','360 - 30','330'],['Sold room-nights','Entered assumption','198'],['Occupancy on defined operating supply','198 / 330','60%'],['Room revenue','198 x 120','23,760 USD'],['ADR','23,760 / 198','120 USD'],['RevPAR on operating supply','23,760 / 330','72 USD']])+p('The <a href="https://www.costar.com/products/str-benchmark/resources/glossary">CoStar STR glossary</a> defines these room-revenue metrics. Here the operating denominator explicitly removes 30 maintenance nights. Against gross physical supply, 198 / 360 = 55% occupancy and $23,760 / 360 = $66 revenue per physical room-night. Do not switch denominators silently or assume your maintenance adjustment matches a benchmark provider\'s reporting rules.')+p('ADR is room revenue divided by sold room-nights. If none are sold, ADR is undefined rather than a meaningful zero-dollar selling rate. Room occupancy and RevPAR do not measure profit, total hotel income or customer satisfaction. The $120 rate is an invented input.')),
('Add costs and a realistic housekeeping constraint',table('Illustrative period economics',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Room revenue','198 x 120','23,760'],['Occupied-night variable costs','198 x 22','4,356'],['Distribution/payment cost assumption','23,760 x 8%','1,900.80'],['Staffing, premises and other listed period costs','Entered total','15,000'],['Remainder before owner pay, financing and tax','23,760 - 4,356 - 1,900.80 - 15,000','2,503.20']])+p('The $22 occupied-night cost and 8% blended distribution/payment assumption are fictional boundaries. An actual plan needs channel-specific agreements, tax handling, utilities, maintenance, replacement spending, debt and owner compensation. A room can be sold only if it is physically available and the required service can be delivered.')+p('Assume 66 departures at 35 cleaning minutes and 132 stayover services at 15 minutes. Housekeeping needs 2,310 + 1,980 = 4,290 minutes, or 71.5 hours. If only 65 hours are available after other duties, the plan is 6.5 hours short. The monthly sum also hides check-out-day peaks, so inspect the daily roster and room turnaround before selling the extra inventory.')),
('Stress demand and collection timing separately',p('At 45% of the same 330 available nights, the arithmetic produces 148.5 nights, which cannot be sold as a count. Use a documented whole-night scenario, such as 149 nights, then recalculate the actual rate: 149 / 330 = 45.15%. Revenue becomes $17,880; occupied-night cost is $3,278 and the 8% fee assumption is $1,430.40. Against the same $15,000 period costs, the remainder is -$1,828.40.')+p('For the original scenario, suppose only $20,000 of the $23,760 earned room revenue has been collected in this period. Opening operating cash of $8,000 plus those receipts minus $21,256.80 of listed payments leaves $6,743.20. The remaining $3,760 is not cash already available. Deposits collected earlier and payments due later need their own dated lines so they are not counted twice.')+p('A maintenance closure can lower available supply without improving cash. Show its repair cost and any lost demand separately; a higher occupancy percentage after removing unavailable rooms is not automatically an improvement in the business.')),
('Build an evidence pack and review before expanding',p('Include a room inventory register, dated channel assumptions, service roster, cost boundary, maintenance plan, receipt schedule and downside response. Verify premises, guest safety, employment, tax and other obligations with the responsible local authorities. This worksheet does not inspect the property or certify compliance. Keep guest identity and payment records outside public planning documents.')+p('Run a limited review comparing forecast with actual sold nights, room revenue, cancellations, service hours and receipts. Investigate the cause before changing a rate or room count. Link the model to the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> and <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">editable narrative template</a>. Use the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">weekly time-blocking worksheet</a> for a manual service-time plan, not as a property-management system.')+p('<strong>Is RevPAR profit?</strong> No, it is room revenue per available room-night. <strong>Can projected fractional room-nights be sold?</strong> No; document the rounding choice. <strong>Does this model prove a hotel is financeable?</strong> No; actual evidence and recipient-specific documents remain necessary.'))
],[SBA,STR],REL)
add('record-label-business-plan','Record Label Business Plan: Releases, Rights & Royalty Cash','Write an independent record-label business plan with a release budget, rights register, statement-to-cash bridge and bounded payout assumptions. No universal per-stream rate claims.','Independent label producer reviewing release folders and headphones at a studio desk',[
('Define what the label actually controls and delivers',p('A record label business plan should state which artists, recordings, territories, services and revenue streams the label is planning. Recording, distribution, marketing, publishing and artist management are different activities. This guide uses a fictional small release campaign and a simplified contractual cash waterfall; it does not establish rights in any real recording.')+p('The <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/engage/musicians/">US Copyright Office explains that musical compositions and sound recordings are separate protected works</a>, commonly owned or licensed separately. Record the actual rights holder, permission, territory, term, approved use and supporting document for each relevant asset. Do not assume that funding a recording grants every right or that a distributor account clears samples, artwork or compositions.')),
('Plan one release before multiplying the catalog',table('Original fictional release budget',['Work package','USD','Acceptance evidence'],[['Recording and mix','1,200','Approved master and agreed delivery specification'],['Artwork and metadata','300','Permissions and final metadata checked'],['Distribution/admin preparation','100','Release identifiers and delivery record'],['Promotion test','900','Bounded campaign, owner and measurement'],['Total cash budget','2,500','1,200 + 300 + 100 + 900']])+p('These are invented costs, not a quote or an industry norm. A real plan should identify who pays, which costs are recoverable under the actual agreement and which are ordinary label overhead. Keep a release ID, version, owner, fixed delivery date and approval trail. A finished track, distributor delivery and public release are separate milestones.')+p('Suppose one release needs 18 hours of coordination, 12 of asset and metadata preparation and 10 of campaign review: 40 hours. If the owner has 60 working hours but reserves 25 for administration and existing catalog work, only 35 remain. The release is five hours over that allowance before unexpected corrections. Revise the timetable or secure capacity instead of assuming two simultaneous releases fit.')),
('Reconcile a statement without inventing a stream rate',table('Illustrative statement and agreed planning waterfall',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Gross reported receipts','Entered statement assumption','4,000'],['Distributor deduction','Fictional 10% x 4,000','400'],['Net amount due to label','4,000 - 400','3,600'],['Recover campaign cash first','Fictional agreement assumption','2,500'],['Residual after recovery','3,600 - 2,500','1,100'],['Artist allocation of residual','Fictional 50% x 1,100','550'],['Label residual after allocation','1,100 - 550','550']])+p('This is one invented contractual sequence, not a standard royalty deal or accounting treatment. It assumes the full listed campaign cash is recoverable first and the residual is then split equally. Another agreement may define a different base, deductions, cross-collateralization, reporting, recovery order or payout. Obtain appropriate review of the actual agreement rather than copying the example.')+p('There is no assumed per-stream rate here. A count of plays does not by itself identify the right, territory, platform, contract deduction or money available. Keep statement period, rights/release ID, gross amount, deductions, payable amounts and actual receipt date linked so duplicate reports are not added as new income.')),
('Show the cash low point and a weaker release',p('With $5,000 opening label operating cash, paying the $2,500 campaign budget before distribution leaves $2,500. After the assumed $3,600 receipt, cash reaches $6,100. Earmarking the $550 artist allocation leaves $5,550 before overhead, owner pay and tax. The difference between the bank amount and unearmarked cash matters; an amount owed to someone else is not unrestricted money for the next campaign.')+p('If gross reported receipts are only $2,000 under the same 10% deduction assumption, the net receipt is $1,800. That is $700 below the $2,500 campaign cash paid. Under this example\'s recovery-first assumption there is no positive residual to split, but actual obligations still follow the real agreement. An unrecouped balance is not automatically money the artist must personally repay.')+p('<a href="https://www.soundexchange.com/faq/how-often-do-i-get-paid/">SoundExchange describes timing and thresholds for its own distributions</a>. That illustrates why statement accrual and bank receipt can differ; it is not the schedule for every distributor, composition royalty or label revenue stream. Use actual expected and received dates in a cash plan.')),
('Choose evidence and a campaign stop rule',p('Document the audience hypothesis, existing artist evidence, release permissions, delivery requirements, campaign channels, spending ceiling and review dates. Distinguish attention metrics from verified receipts. A campaign can attract plays without recovering its cash, and a catalog can have future potential without funding a payment due today.')+p('After release, reconcile the approved budget, actual hours, statements, deductions, artist obligations and collections. Preserve the original plan and explain revisions. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">planning guide</a> organizes the narrative; the <a href="/tools/content-publishing-checklist/">publishing checklist</a> can record a manual asset review but does not clear music rights or distribute a recording.')+p('<strong>Does paying studio costs establish ownership?</strong> Not from this worksheet. <strong>Does a positive statement mean cash arrived?</strong> No, reconcile receipts. <strong>Can every royalty stream be combined without checking rights?</strong> No; keep each stream and contractual boundary explicit.'))
],[SBA,COPY,SX],REL)
add('travel-agency-business-plan','Travel Agency Business Plan: Booking Value, Fees & Cash','Build a travel agency business plan that separates trip booking value, client and supplier funds, earned agency fees, service hours and refund exposure. Includes a fictional example.','Travel advisor reviewing anonymous booking folders beside a globe and route cards',[
('Specify the travel service and who receives the money',p('A travel agency business plan should define its customer, destinations, services, supplier relationships and money flow. A planning-only advisor, a package seller and an airfare ticket agent can have different duties and economics. This guide uses a fictional agency arranging trips with agreed service fees and assumed supplier commissions; it does not establish accreditation or permission to handle client money.')+p('Map who contracts with the traveler, who is merchant of record, where payment goes, who owes each service and who handles changes or refunds. Verify the requirements for the actual activity and location. Do not describe a booking system or supplier relationship as active without evidence. The plan must keep customer travel funds separate from the agency\'s own earned income and operating forecast.')),
('Separate booking volume from the agency revenue model',table('Original fictional ten-trip period',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Gross trip booking value','10 x 2,000','20,000'],['Assumed supplier-related amount','10 x 1,800','18,000'],['Assumed agency commission','20,000 - 18,000','2,000'],['Separately agreed service fees','10 x 100','1,000'],['Modeled agency income','2,000 + 1,000','3,000']])+p('The $20,000 trip value is not $20,000 of agency operating income in this simplified agency model. The $2,000 commission is a fictional contract assumption, not a universal margin; its eligibility, base, timing and reversals must be checked. The $100 service fee is also invented and must be considered within actual agreements and applicable requirements.')+p('Do not double-count a service fee embedded in the trip value and then add it again as separate income. This example explicitly treats the ten $100 fees as outside the $20,000 supplier-booking value. A different principal-versus-agent business model may require a different reporting boundary and professional assessment.')),
('Count changes and support in service capacity',p('Assume each of the ten trips needs three hours to plan and arrange, plus one hour of expected changes or traveler support. That is 40 direct hours. With 60 total owner hours and 15 reserved for administration and supplier reconciliation, the direct allowance is 45 hours and the buffer is five.')+p('Two disrupted itineraries needing four additional hours each raise direct work to 48 hours, three beyond the allowance. That is a staffing and service-coverage problem even if the original bookings looked profitable. Record travel dates, emergency coverage arrangements, handoffs and the boundaries of the promised service; a generic monthly average can conceal simultaneous departures.')+table('Fictional agency operating costs',['Line','USD'],[['Direct communication and delivery costs','10 x 20 = 200'],['Period overhead','900'],['Modeled remainder before owner pay and tax','3,000 - 200 - 900 = 1,900']])+p('The remainder excludes owner compensation, tax and unlisted costs. Add actual payment fees, insurance, professional support, refunds and chargeback exposure as appropriate to the real money flow. Use the <a href="/tools/time-blocking-template/">weekly time-blocking worksheet</a> for manual support coverage; it is not a booking engine or travel alert service.')),
('Build the cash bridge without spending client funds',p('Assume the agency has collected its $1,000 service fees but received only $800 of its $2,000 commission assumption this period. Its own operating receipts are $1,800; $1,200 remains expected later. Starting with $4,000 of operating cash and paying $1,100 of listed costs leaves $4,700. If the owner earmarks $600 for possible reversals or service refunds, unearmarked operating cash is $4,100. That earmark is an invented planning input, not a legal reserve rule.')+p('Separately, suppose $18,000 due to suppliers is temporarily held pending onward payment. It must not be added to the $4,100 as a source of marketing or owner withdrawals. Maintain an appropriate reconciliation and follow the actual requirements. The <a href="https://www.oag.ca.gov/travel/reg-faqs">California Attorney General seller-of-travel FAQs</a> give a California-specific example of client-fund safeguards; they are not universal rules for every agency.')+p('For a weaker-demand scenario of eight trips, modeled agency income becomes $2,400. Direct costs become $160 while overhead stays $900, leaving $1,340 before the same omissions. Separately model a delayed commission or a reversal after a cancellation. Lower volume and slower collection are different stresses.')),
('Document refund responsibilities and the review loop',p('The <a href="https://www.transportation.gov/individuals/aviation-consumer-protection/refunds">US DOT refund guidance</a> explains ticket-agent responsibilities within its airfare scope, including the importance of merchant-of-record status. Do not turn that into a blanket rule for every hotel, package, service fee or jurisdiction. Check the actual booking and refund conditions, responsible party and applicable requirements before accepting payment.')+p('Keep a dated evidence index for supplier terms, client consent, disclosures, service scope, money handling and expected commission statements. During a limited trial, reconcile booking IDs, changes, support hours, fees earned, money collected and open obligations. Store traveler identity and payment records in controlled systems, not a public business plan.')+p('Use the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> for the wider structure and <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> for evidence owners. <strong>Is booking value the same as income?</strong> Not in this model. <strong>Is expected commission already available cash?</strong> No. <strong>Does a planning worksheet establish refund compliance?</strong> No; actual responsibilities need their own assessment.'))
],[SBA,DOT,CA],REL)
add('bakery-business-plan','Bakery Business Plan: Batch Yield, Waste & Cash Example','Write a bakery business plan using batches, saleable yield, sell-through, production time, ingredient costs and cash timing. Includes an original downside and stock reconciliation.','Bakery owner reviewing a paper batch sheet and calculator beside bread trays',[
('Choose the bakery format and selling unit',p('A bakery business plan starts with the actual operation: a home-based order business, neighborhood shop, wholesale producer and cafe have different capacity, delivery and regulatory needs. This guide models a fictional bread-batch operation selling one clearly defined unit. It does not provide a food-safety process, recipe approval or permission to sell a particular product.')+p('Define the buyer, product range, pack/unit size, order channel, production window, delivery or collection arrangement and evidence of demand. Start with a bounded product mix before multiplying expected sales across many lines. A busy display or social audience does not establish paid sell-through, and unsold units are not revenue.')),
('Reconcile batch yield and physical sell-through',table('Original fictional production period',['Line','Calculation','Units'],[['Production batches','Entered plan','10'],['Units per batch','Entered yield','40'],['Units produced','10 x 40','400'],['Quality rejects / production loss','Entered observation assumption','20'],['Saleable units','400 - 20','380'],['Units sold','Entered assumption','342'],['Unsold saleable units','380 - 342','38']])+p('Production yield is 380 / 400 = 95%. Sell-through of saleable output is 342 / 380 = 90%. Sold share of all produced units is 342 / 400 = 85.5%. These describe different stages; do not swap denominators to make the result appear stronger. The unsold 38 units still need a documented disposition, date and quality assessment.')+p('Assume six oven hours are available and each batch occupies 30 minutes: ten batches use five hours, leaving one hour. If each batch also requires 45 labor minutes, the work needs 7.5 labor hours. Seven available labor hours leave a 0.5-hour gap even though oven time fits. Cooling, packing, cleaning and simultaneous equipment limits can reduce practical output further.')),
('Model the period contribution with a clear cost boundary',table('Illustrative sales and spending view',['Line','Calculation','USD'],[['Sales','342 x 4','1,368'],['Ingredients for all ten batches','10 x 28','280'],['Packaging on sold units','342 x 0.30','102.60'],['Listed period labor','Entered total','300'],['Utilities allocation','Entered total','60'],['Premises and other listed period cost','Entered total','250'],['Sales less listed spending','1,368 - 992.60','375.40']])+p('The $4 price and $28 batch ingredient cost are fictional, not market averages. This deliberately charges ingredients for the whole production period, including unsold and rejected units. It is a bounded spending/contribution view rather than a formal inventory-valued income statement. Do not also deduct those same ingredients again when accounting for rejects.')+p('The $375.40 remainder is before owner pay, tax, financing and omitted costs. An actual plan may need a different cost-of-sales and inventory treatment, equipment replacement and other expenses. Keep purchasing, consumption, production loss, sales and cash receipts as separate records. The <a href="/journal/farmers-market-business-plan/">market-stall plan</a> adds an event selling-channel view; it is not a replacement for bakery production controls.')),
('Test lower demand before raising production',p('If only 300 of the same 380 saleable units sell, revenue falls to $1,200 and sold-unit packaging to $90. Ingredients remain $280 because all ten batches were already made. With the same $300 labor, $60 utilities and $250 other costs, the remainder is $220. Unsold saleable units rise to 80. Lower sell-through does not automatically undo the earlier ingredient purchase or production work.')+p('Suppose the original 342-unit scenario earns $1,368 but collects only $1,200 during the period, leaving $168 outstanding. With $2,000 opening operating cash and $992.60 of listed payments, closing cash is $2,207.40. Any unsold product is not cash in the bank; customer payment timing must also be checked.')+p('Use a dated preorder and batch register to test the demand hypothesis, but do not assume orders are noncancelable or collected unless the evidence shows it. Compare actual sell-through by product and day. A smaller batch, different production day or reduced range may be a better next test than simply adding an oven.')),
('Verify product requirements and preserve the evidence',p('The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-industry/how-start-food-business">FDA food-business overview</a> explains that requirements depend on the product and facility and involve relevant federal, state and local authorities. A private-residence registration exclusion is not blanket permission to sell any home-baked food. Confirm the actual operation\'s permissions, labeling and handling requirements with the responsible authorities; this worksheet does not inspect or certify them.')+p('Create a plan with customer evidence, unit and batch definitions, production constraints, ingredient and packaging basis, loss records, costs, dated receipts/payments and a downside response. Keep sensitive customer and supplier records out of public notes. Use the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> to connect the sections and the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">editable template</a> to record the narrative.')+p('During a four-week permitted trial, retain the initial forecast and review actual batches, rejects, unsold units, labor hours and collections. Name an owner, review date and spending ceiling. <strong>Are all produced units sales?</strong> No. <strong>Does unused oven time prove spare production capacity?</strong> No; labor and other constraints may bind first. <strong>Is the remainder take-home profit?</strong> No; the cost boundary and omitted items remain explicit.'))
],[SBA,FDA],REL)
